WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:01.373
- [Narrator] The polls heading

00:00:01.373 --> 00:00:03.960
into the Iowa Republican
caucuses look like,

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:05.280
well, Trump winning.

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:08.550
But what's interesting is
what's happening down here.

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:10.200
So we asked our political editor -

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:13.200
- You can write Aaron
Zitner Wonk if you want.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:15.030
- [Narrator] to explain
what's really happening

00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:17.310
in the first stop of
the Republican primary.

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:19.350
- Well, the race in Iowa is largely about

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:22.290
who comes in second and by how much.

00:00:22.290 --> 00:00:25.650
It's gonna give us the
first clues in actual votes,

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:28.260
not in polls or in media attention

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:30.390
to whether the Republican
Party really is ready

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:33.120
to consolidate behind Donald Trump.

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:34.620
- [Narrator] Here's why the Iowa caucus

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:36.180
will likely determine how the rest

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:37.983
of the Republican primary will go.

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:41.460
- Let's look at some of the scenarios

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:42.990
that could come out of Iowa.

00:00:42.990 --> 00:00:47.550
First and most likely,
Donald Trump dominates.

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:49.080
That would give us a lot of signals about

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:51.540
where the Republican Party is right now.

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:52.890
- [Narrator] Because the party in Iowa

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:54.840
hasn't gathered behind Trump.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:56.640
Ron desantis has gotten the endorsement

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:59.250
of Iowa's very popular Republican governor

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:02.790
and arguably the biggest
evangelical leader in the state,

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:05.910
and he's visited all 99 counties.

00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:09.060
Nikki Haley, meanwhile, has
been campaigning hard in Iowa

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:11.130
and is set up to spend the most in ads

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:13.260
leading up to the caucus.
- Generation.

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:15.930
- If Donald Trump runs
away with the race in Iowa,

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:19.590
that will tell us that even
influential Republicans

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:22.770
don't have the juice to
persuade Republican voters

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:24.570
that it's time to turn the page

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:27.120
and to move on to another leader.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:31.680
A second possibility, Donald Trump wins,

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:35.220
but by less of a margin,
and the second place

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:39.510
is kind of a tie between Ron
DeSantis and Nikki Haley.

00:01:39.510 --> 00:01:42.300
That's also a good
outcome for Donald Trump

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:43.770
because it will mean that voters

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:46.110
who don't want him haven't consolidated

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:49.020
around any individual
contender against him

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:50.580
and instead, are dividing their vote

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:52.350
among multiple candidates.

00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:54.300
- [Narrator] That happened in 2016.

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:57.090
Ted Cruz and Marco Rubio
split the non-Trump vote

00:01:57.090 --> 00:01:58.260
in a bunch of states.

00:01:58.260 --> 00:01:59.970
But that just allowed Trump to win

00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:03.450
and rack up the delegate count
needed for the nomination.

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:05.940
- And then we come to a third scenario.

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:09.120
And that's the one where
a single candidate emerges

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:11.490
from Iowa with enough momentum

00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:15.570
to give Donald Trump a real
race throughout the primaries.

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:17.520
- [Narrator] If one
candidate does well in Iowa,

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:20.340
it would signal to voters
in other states and donors

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:22.650
that they're a viable contender.

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:26.910
Just like it did for Trump
in 2016 or Romney in 2012,

00:02:26.910 --> 00:02:28.650
they both came in second in Iowa,

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:30.450
but won New Hampshire next.

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:32.970
And eventually, the nomination.

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:34.650
And that's not unusual.

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:37.893
Whoever wins Iowa usually
doesn't become the nominee.

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:40.380
It doesn't help that Iowa is.

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:42.510
- Very agricultural and very rural.

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:44.310
But there are some places
that could give us clues

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:47.250
to what might be coming
next in the primary process.

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:49.740
And we'll look for those here and here,

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:52.830
right around Des Moines and
in the two university towns

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:54.930
just north of Des Moines and over here

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:56.550
at the University of Iowa.

00:02:56.550 --> 00:02:59.940
They're upper income, they
are more highly educated

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:02.700
and that means, they
look a little bit more

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:05.610
like the state of New
Hampshire, which votes next.

00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:08.250
If Nikki Haley and Ron
Desantis don't do well

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:12.180
in these areas that are
suburban and university-heavy,

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:14.760
it's a signal that they
might not be doing well

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:16.980
in the states that come next.

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:18.230
- And there's another reason Haley

00:03:18.230 --> 00:03:20.670
or DeSantis might want
to stay in the race.

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:23.490
The numerous legal battles
Trump is currently facing.

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:24.960
- They don't want to have dropped out

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:26.790
if something unexpected happens

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:28.740
and all of a sudden
Donald Trump isn't there

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:30.093
to claim the nomination.

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:32.850
- [Narrator] Iowa hasn't been a kingmaker.

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:35.070
Instead, it's been a win over,

00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:37.080
defining who the top candidates are going

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:39.750
to be in the primary from a large field

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:42.540
and boosting the profile
of those that do well.

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:44.850
In 2024, it will do it again.

00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:46.860
But whether it windows it to one, two,

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:49.260
or three candidates will also show us

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:52.353
if this primary will be
over in one month or six.

00:03:53.688 --> 00:03:56.271
(bright music)

